response:
Loops are used to repeatedly execute a block of code.  You can use loops of different types to perform different sorts of actions.  For example:

for (int i = 0; i < 10; i++) {
    printf("%d ", i);
}

This will print the integers 0 through 9 to the console on a new line for each iteration of the loop.  Another type of loop is the "while" loop.  Here’s an example:

while (x < 10) {
    printf("%d ", x);
    x++;
}

This will print the integers 0 through 9 to the console for each iteration of the loop.  The loop condition, here  x < 10, is checked at the beginning of each loop iteration.  The loop body is executed at least once, and then the loop condition is checked again.  If the condition is true, then the loop body is executed again, and the process continues.  If the condition is false, the loop stops and the program continues with the next instructions.

